      "output": {
        "message": "Here are three practical ways you can handle this, depending on how you want to approach it: 1. **Document your specific work first.** Before doing anything else, pull together timestamps, file drafts, emails, or commit logs that clearly show what you created or led. Having a clean record makes any follow-up objective rather than a personal dispute. 2. **Talk directly to your coworker.** If there's a chance it was an oversight or poor phrasing, you can address it privately: *\"During the update, it came across like the entire build was yours. Moving forward, let's make sure both our contributions are clearly called out.\"* Notice how they respond—whether they offer to correct it or get defensive. 3. **Establish your ownership with your manager.** In your next 1-on-1, or via a brief project wrap-up email, share a breakdown of the specific deliverables you owned and the results. You don't have to frame it as an accusation; you can frame it as reporting your performance and next steps. Which of those feels like the best fit for your workplace?",
